saphira-openstack - Saphira native OpenStack installer foundation
=================================================================

This package contains a menu-driven installer foundation for designing an
OpenStack cloud on Saphira Linux. It installs TOOLING ONLY. It does not
install, configure or imply any OpenStack service, and `apk add
saphira-openstack` modifies nothing on the machine.

Three interfaces over one engine:

  saphira-openstack init [--from FILE] [--output FILE]
      Interactive terminal wizard. You design the cloud through menus:
      topology, networking, storage, services, operations. Every choice is
      yours; Back, Review and Cancel are always available. The wizard may
      read the local machine read-only (interfaces, OVS bridges, VLANs,
      ZFS pools, routes, init system) and offer detected values as
      suggestions - never as decisions. Nothing is created, formatted,
      adopted or modified without your explicit confirmation. On confirm,
      the wizard writes an inventory document. Nothing is applied.
      --from loads an existing document as the editable base: every
      choice is shown, kept unless explicitly changed, and a keep-all
      run reproduces the input plan byte-for-byte.

  saphira-openstack inventory [--file PATH] [--check]
      Inspect and validate the inventory document. Read-only.

  saphira-openstack plan [--file PATH] [--out DIR]
      Display exactly what would be installed or changed, without changing
      the system. Writes only into an explicit --out directory.

Design rules
------------

  * No hardcoded addresses, domains, hostnames, interfaces, bridges, VLANs
    or storage devices. Examples illustrate; they are never defaults.
  * IPv4 and IPv6 are independent everywhere. IPv4-only, IPv6-only and
    dual-stack are all first-class.
  * Existing networks and storage are used as-is; nothing is recreated,
    formatted or destroyed without explicit approval.
  * One network may serve several OpenStack purposes where the
    configuration supports it.
  * The inventory document is editable, exportable and reproducible; the
    plan carries a plan_id so the same document always yields the same
    plan.

Status of OpenStack services
-----------------------------

Readiness is derived, never maintained. The component catalog maps each
service to its provider APK; `inventory` and `plan` report a service as
ready only when that package is installed or available in a configured
apk repository here, and pending otherwise (no native package, or not
obtainable from here). The wizard lets you design a complete cloud -
the plan marks each component honestly and never claims an
uninstallable service is deployable. Installing this package does not
install OpenStack.

For hermetic testing, SAPHIRA_OPENSTACK_PROVIDES names a file listing
the available APKs instead of probing the live package manager.

Files
-----

  /usr/sbin/saphira-openstack           the tool
  /usr/libexec/saphira/openstack/      engine, wizard, inventory, plan, detect
  /usr/share/saphira/openstack/        catalogs, examples, format doc
  /usr/share/doc/saphira-openstack/    this README
  /etc/saphira-openstack/inventory.ini where init writes (only on confirm)
